# Chat templates in the choice fixtures are pinned by SHA-256 and rendered
# byte for byte in tests, so check them out exactly as committed: no CRLF
# conversion under core.autocrlf on Windows.
integration-tests/fixtures/choice/templates/** -text

# build.rs compares the raw bytes of these three files with the hashes pinned
# in natives-manifest.txt before it downloads a prebuilt llama.cpp slice. A
# CRLF checkout never matches, so Windows checkouts always built llama.cpp
# from source.
crates/llama-cpp-sys/build.rs -text
crates/llama-cpp-sys/wrapper.cpp -text
crates/llama-cpp-sys/wrapper.h -text

# The decision conformance fixtures are pinned byte for byte (case files,
# oracle.json, schema.json), and the decision oracle's sources are hashed into
# the identity stamped into its executables and recorded in oracle.json. Check
# both out exactly as committed: no CRLF conversion under core.autocrlf.
integration-tests/fixtures/decision/** -text
tools/conformance/decision/** -text

# The HF reference's tooling is hashed byte for byte too: pins.json and
# requirements.txt, and every recipe source (run_reference.py, make_cases.py,
# provenance.py, ...) whose sha256 its runs and provenance.json record. A
# converted checkout would fail provenance.py --check. No CRLF conversion.
tools/conformance/decision_hf/** -text
